00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:27.400
Here is how it works when a tournament, not a market, is the aggregation.

00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:33.560
The approach that won a US intelligence community geopolitical forecasting tournament was to

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:37.500
pull the best performers out each year and put them on elite teams.

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:43.640
A 2015 paper in Perspectives on Psychological Science names the people on those teams

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:45.000
super forecasters.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.720
They stayed accurate across hundreds of questions and a wide spread of topics.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:55.220
They did that two years running against the usual expectation that extreme scores fall

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:56.460
back toward the average.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.660
The authors see four supports working together.

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:06.500
how people think, skills built for the task, motivation and commitment, and a richer setting to work in.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.640
On their account, these forecasters are found and also made.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:17.560
Tournament incentives, the paper says, surface judgment that lab studies of ordinary performance would miss.

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:21.740
A 2014 paper in psychological science sets the scene.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.540
Five university research groups spent two years recruiting forecasters,

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.260
Collecting predictions and turning them into probabilities for geopolitical events.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:36.720
One group tested training, teaming, and tracking, and found support for all three.

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:42.740
Training pushed back on cognitive biases, steered people toward reference classes and

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:47.320
taught shortcuts, including an average when several estimates were already on the table.

00:04:48.040 --> 00:04:51.720
Teaming meant sharing information and arguing the reasons for a belief.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:57.400
Tracking put the top 2% from the first year onto elite teams that kept working together.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:02.560
Training, collaboration, and that tracking step improved calibration and resolution.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.460
Statistical algorithms, described in other work, made the combined number more accurate.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:14.280
Using the statistics and the psychology together produced the best forecasts two years in a row.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.260
Those papers do not show that a contract price automatically picks up that record.

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:25.360
They do show accuracy moved when selection, training, teams, and a statistical combination

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:26.460
rule changed.

